  "sourceFitAssessment": {
    "learnerTask": "Connect a specific normal-versus-altered sequence to a candidate peptide while retaining separate presentation and recognition evidence.",
    "gap": "The evidence table distinguishes the gates well but does not show how an altered sequence changes a candidate fragment. A small normal/altered sequence inset can reuse the antigen-processing owner rather than duplicating its full pathway.",
    "accuracyGuardrails": [
      "An alteration can yield several candidates or none useful.",
      "An unusual transcript is not automatically tumor-exclusive.",
      "Sequence-specific novelty does not establish recognition, safety or benefit."
    ],
    "baselinePageSHA256": "cde2f028b44b347c03513a64be3e10dfcb5ff107062b3f2263ad01017d32dcce",
    "currentVisualFit": "adequate",
    "humanReview": "pending",
    "reasoning": "Matched normal and altered abstract protein strips differ at one marked block, and the smaller candidate includes that block. Display and compatible receptor response are independently posed as questions, with dashed transition and an explicit no-benefit/no-safety limit. The retained processing diagram supplies the common route; the new comparison supplies the normal-versus-altered view and evidence gaps missing from the frozen task. No actual sequence or mutation selection instructions are supplied."
  },
